{"duration_api_ms":19034,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b79370bc-83ec-40aa-8acb-219332ecf686","total_cost_usd":0.05878,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2227,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":158},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1779,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2227,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1779},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":2227,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":1779,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.05878,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":158,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"# Presentation Outline: Harness Engineering and the Multi-Agent Workflow Builder MVP\n\n**Duration:** 15 minutes. The sections below add up to 15:00.\n\n**Topics used:** `harness_engineering`, `multi_agent_builder` and `workflow_template`. The query names Harness Engineering explicitly, so I added it to the two default topics.\n\n---\n\n## Section 1: Opening (0:00–2:00, 2 min)\n\n**Goal:** Frame the talk around what an agent is and where this project fits.\n\n- **Slide 1:** Title slide.\n- **Slide 2:** Define **Agent = Model + Harness**.\n\n**Evidence points:**\n- Harness Engineering is the execution structure around an agent. It includes tools, memory, constraints, verification and feedback loops. *(harness_engineering)*\n- The talk will move from the generic harness to project-specific value: workflow templates and evidence. *(harness_engineering)*\n\n---\n\n## Section 2: Problem (2:00–4:30, 2.5 min)\n\n**Goal:** State the challenge the MVP has to answer.\n\n- **Slide 3:** A harness makes multi-agent development easier. So what value remains beyond a generic harness?\n- **Slide 4:** Split the harness into generic parts and project-specific parts.\n\n**Evidence points:**\n- A harness can make multi-agent development easier, so a project must explain what value remains beyond using a generic harness. *(harness_engineering)*\n- The differentiating value should move from generic execution plumbing to domain-specific workflow templates and evidence. *(harness_engineering)*\n- **Defensive positioning sentence (draft, based only on the supplied points):** *\"Generic harnesses provide the execution plumbing; this MVP adds domain-specific workflow templates that become executable Workflow JSON with traceable evidence.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Section 3: Architecture (4:30–9:00, 4.5 min)\n\n**Goal:** Show how a template becomes an executable, verifiable workflow.\n\n- **Slide 5:** Pipeline diagram: **Template → Workflow JSON → Engine → Trace**.\n- **Slide 6:** What a template defines: node order, node roles, required fields and builder equivalents.\n- **Slide 7:** Workflow JSON as the contract between the visual builder and the execution engine.\n- **Slide 8:** Sequential execution plus trace output as the explainability layer.\n\n**Evidence points:**\n- A workflow builder turns a domain process into ordered nodes with defined inputs and outputs. *(multi_agent_builder)*\n- Templates generate executable Workflow JSON instead of staying as static diagrams. *(multi_agent_builder)*\n- A template defines node order, node roles, required fields and builder equivalents. *(workflow_template)*\n- The generated Workflow JSON is the contract between the visual builder and the execution engine. *(workflow_template)*\n- Sequential execution and trace output make the result explainable during a presentation. *(multi_agent_builder)*\n\n---\n\n## Section 4: Multi-Domain Evidence and Demo (9:00–13:00, 4 min)\n\n**Goal:** Show that the builder is not hard-coded to one scenario, and prove that templates execute.\n\n- **Slide 9:** Side-by-side comparison of templates from different domains.\n- **Slide 10:** Highlight the common structure and the domain-specific nodes.\n- **Live demo (under 2 min):** Select a template, run it, then show the trace.\n- **Slide 11:** Use the trace as proof of execution: expected node order and intermediate evidence.\n\n**Evidence points:**\n- Using multiple domains shows the builder is not hard-coded to one recommendation scenario. *(multi_agent_builder)*\n- Comparing templates across domains reveals the shared structure and the domain-specific nodes. *(workflow_template)*\n- The trace verifies that each node ran in the expected order and produced intermediate evidence. *(workflow_template)*\n- Keep the demo under two minutes by showing only template selection and the trace. *(multi_agent_builder)*\n\n**⚠️ Missing information for this section:**\n- **Domain names.** The data only implies that a recommendation scenario exists. No other domains are named.\n- **Template contents.** No per-domain node lists, roles or required fields are given, so the Slide 9–10 comparison cannot be filled in.\n- **Trace output.** No sample trace, node logs or intermediate evidence are provided.\n- **Results.** No test results, success rates or performance numbers are provided.\n\nUntil you supply these, the slides should use placeholders such as *[Domain A template]*, *[Domain B template]* and *[Sample trace]* rather than invented examples.\n\n---\n\n## Section 5: Conclusion (13:00–15:00, 2 min)\n\n**Goal:** Restate the positioning and close.\n\n- **Slide 12:** Three takeaways.\n- **Slide 13:** Q&A.\n\n**Evidence points (takeaways):**\n1. **Agent = Model + Harness.** Generic harnesses make multi-agent development easier. *(harness_engineering)*\n2. **The value is in the templates.** Domain-specific templates generate executable Workflow JSON, which is the contract between builder and engine. *(harness_engineering, workflow_template)*\n3. **The trace is the proof.** Sequential execution with trace output shows the expected node order and intermediate evidence across domains. *(multi_agent_builder, workflow_template)*\n- Repeat the defensive positioning sentence from Section 2.\n\n---\n\n## Timing Summary\n\n| Section | Time | Duration |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opening | 0:00–2:00 | 2.0 min |\n| Problem | 2:00–4:30 | 2.5 min |\n| Architecture | 4:30–9:00 | 4.5 min |\n| Multi-Domain Evidence and Demo | 9:00–13:00 | 4.0 min |\n| Conclusion | 13:00–15:00 | 2.0 min |\n| **Total** | | **15 min** |\n\n**Source note:** Every evidence point comes from the supplied topic data. No domains, metrics, trace contents or other facts were invented.","ttft_ms":2810,"type":"result","duration_ms":19070,"uuid":"704322cb-a4d2-4f77-8673-066bd7c7941b","ttft_stream_ms":1380,"time_to_request_ms":34,"first_content_frame_ms":1381,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
